# ==============================================================================================
# pins.env - EVERY version / digest / path / option pin used by tools/dx-native-tools.
# Edit here only. build.sh, container-build.sh, export-sysroot.sh and functional-gate.sh source
# this file and echo the resolved values at the top of every run, so a build log always says
# exactly what it used. The Windows and macOS scripts read its platform-neutral keys too
# (TOOL_SOURCES_DIR, RUST_TOOLCHAIN, DX_*, WASM_BINDGEN_*, LIBGIT2_*, LICENCE_FILES), so every
# platform builds and gates the same thing.
# ==============================================================================================

# --- WHERE THE SOURCE IS - the one setting to change when the trees move ----------------------
# The folder, relative to the REPOSITORY ROOT, that holds the five things the two tools are
# built from:
#     <TOOL_SOURCES_DIR>/dioxus-cli/          the Dioxus CLI source (dioxus-cli 0.7.10 with the
#                                             repository's patches applied)
#     <TOOL_SOURCES_DIR>/codebrix-libgit2-sys/ the repository's declarations-only stand-in for
#                                             the libgit2-sys crate (dx's Cargo.toml substitutes
#                                             it; it links the prebuilt libgit2 below)
#     <TOOL_SOURCES_DIR>/wasm-bindgen-cli/    the wasm-bindgen CLI source (0.2.130, unpatched)
#     <TOOL_SOURCES_DIR>/vendor/              every crate the two need (written by cargo vendor)
#     <TOOL_SOURCES_DIR>/.cargo/config.toml   points cargo at vendor/ instead of crates.io
# The trees are READ, never written: the Linux build mounts this folder read-only and puts
# cargo's target folder in the container's scratch; the Windows and macOS scripts set
# CARGO_TARGET_DIR outside it. When the trees move (planned: to the repository root), change
# this line and nothing else.
TOOL_SOURCES_DIR=tools/dx-native-tools/sources

# --- Container images (Linux) -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The manylinux images exist to fix the glibc baseline. Building on a modern desktop distro bakes
# THAT distro's glibc version into the executable, and glibc symbol versioning is forward-only,
# so the tool would then refuse to start on anything older - a failure that only shows up on a
# user's machine.
#
#   image                                  glibc  runs on
#   manylinux_2_28_x86_64                  2.28   Debian 10+, Ubuntu 20.04+, RHEL 8+
#   manylinux_2_28_aarch64                 2.28   every 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS, Ubuntu 20.04+
#
# The same two images, at the same dated tag and the same digests, that
# CodeBrix.WebPlatform.WasmShrink (and CodeBrix.LibGit2, CodeBrix.WebGpu, CodeBrix.Sdl3 ...) pin,
# so every CodeBrix Linux native comes from one base userland. The digest is what the build
# pins; build.sh resolves it, so an upstream retag cannot change the toolchain under a rebuild.
IMAGE_X64=quay.io/pypa/manylinux_2_28_x86_64:2026.07.19-1
IMAGE_X64_DIGEST=sha256:a61875a2f84cab7df8de222ff12cabc08ff86eb4ad402ac90ba7bdaed9600cca
IMAGE_ARM64=quay.io/pypa/manylinux_2_28_aarch64:2026.07.19-1
IMAGE_ARM64_DIGEST=sha256:162c81dfd3efc710732a571717d3c916a6945ebf279e879ddee3243af96fe46f

# ONE derived image builds both Linux RIDs: the x86_64 base + clang/lld/llvm (the cross linker
# and llvm-strip), `file`, cpio, the OpenSSL development headers (OPENSSL_DEVEL_VERSION below),
# rustup + RUST_TOOLCHAIN + the aarch64 target, and the aarch64 CROSS SYSROOT exported from the
# pinned aarch64 image (export-sysroot.sh) with the same OpenSSL headers added for aarch64. linux-arm64 is therefore
# CROSS-COMPILED - the route CodeBrix.WebGpu took for its Rust natives - and only RUN (the
# `--version` gate) in the pinned aarch64 base image under qemu. Building the derived image is
# the ONE step that uses the network; every build afterwards runs with --network none.
DERIVED_IMAGE=codebrix-dx-build-x86_64

# The OpenSSL DEVELOPMENT HEADERS the derived image installs (x86_64 with dnf; aarch64 unpacked
# from the distribution's aarch64 packages into the cross sysroot), at this exact release - the
# openssl-libs release both pinned base images already carry. WHY: dx's project templates
# (`dx new`, `dx init`) come from the cargo-generate crate, which turns on the `https` feature of
# the git2 crate, and on Linux that feature makes git2 depend on the openssl-sys crate. git2
# never calls it, so NOTHING from OpenSSL is compiled into or linked by dx (the gate proves it:
# no libssl / libcrypto in NEEDED, no OpenSSL symbol imported) - but openssl-sys's build script
# reads the headers to learn the OpenSSL version, and stops without them. A bare-host build on
# Debian needs `sudo apt install libssl-dev` for the same reason. Not needed on Windows or macOS.
OPENSSL_DEVEL_VERSION=1.1.1k-17.el8_6

# --- Rust toolchain ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Exactly this release, on every platform (the derived Linux image installs it; the Windows and
# macOS scripts run `cargo +<this>` and refuse to start without it). It is the toolchain the
# repository's other Rust code is checked against (CodeBrix.WebPlatform.FrontendBuilder's
# minimum).
RUST_TOOLCHAIN=1.100.0

# --- The two tools ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Built with (the command the WorkSite sample's build program used before the host-tools
# packages replaced its own build):
#     cargo build --release --locked --offline [--features ...]
# plus --target <triple> (so build scripts and procedural macros are untouched by RUSTFLAGS) and
# the path-remapping flags container-build.sh sets (no effect on generated code).
DX_DIR=dioxus-cli
DX_CRATE_VERSION=0.7.10
DX_FEATURES=no-downloads,disable-telemetry
# dx's build.rs bakes a git commit into `dx --version`, taken from the FIRST .git folder above
# the source - which, for a source tree inside this repository, is THIS repository's commit (a
# build from a working copy printed "dioxus 0.7.10 (b0c0f1e)", our own commit at the time). Set
# explicitly to upstream's commit for dioxus-cli 0.7.10 (dioxus-cli/.cargo_vcs_info.json, which
# the gate checks it against) so every platform's dx reports the same, true thing.
DX_GIT_SHA=57d6794ad60b949e5bd8aa282f6f8c3dc97a365e
DX_EXPECTED_VERSION="dioxus 0.7.10 (57d6794)"
DX_EXE=dx

WASM_BINDGEN_DIR=wasm-bindgen-cli
WASM_BINDGEN_VERSION=0.2.130
WASM_BINDGEN_FEATURES=
WASM_BINDGEN_EXPECTED_VERSION="wasm-bindgen 0.2.130"
# The package builds three programs (wasm-bindgen, wasm-bindgen-test-runner, wasm2es6js), as it
# always has; only wasm-bindgen is shipped.
WASM_BINDGEN_EXE=wasm-bindgen

# --- libgit2: dx LINKS it; it is NOT built here and NOT committed here --------------------------
# dx's git features (`dx new`, `dx init`, `dx components`) use the git2 crate, which here builds
# on the declarations-only stand-in codebrix-libgit2-sys. The stand-in links the PREBUILT
# libgit2 that the published NuGet package below ships, as a shared library. Every platform's
# build takes it out of that package: from the local NuGet cache if the exact package is there,
# otherwise downloaded from nuget.org into output/libgit2/ (build scratch, git-ignored) - the
# Linux and macOS builds with linux/fetch-libgit2.sh, the Windows build in build.dx.ps1. The
# package file and every library file are checked against the checksums below; the network is
# used only when the package is not already in the cache or in output/libgit2/.
# dx then NEEDS the library at run time and DOES NOT START without it (README.txt, "dx NEEDS
# libgit2 TO START"): on Linux the file under its SONAME on LD_LIBRARY_PATH; on macOS the dylib
# under its install name's leaf on DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH; on Windows the DLL beside dx.exe or on PATH.
LIBGIT2_PACKAGE_ID=CodeBrix.LibGit2.MitLicenseForever
LIBGIT2_PACKAGE_VERSION=1.0.323.362
LIBGIT2_NUPKG_SHA256=0f1365422bbce31348e269fdd555e65414a18fdb348e61f84a53156fa2f0d87c
LIBGIT2_NUPKG_URL=https://api.nuget.org/v3-flatcontainer/codebrix.libgit2.mitlicenseforever/1.0.323.362/codebrix.libgit2.mitlicenseforever.1.0.323.362.nupkg
# libgit2 1.9.7 (tag v1.9.7 = commit 49e408b3), as that package names its native files. dx's
# git2 0.20.4 targets libgit2 1.9 (libgit2-sys 0.18.3+1.9.2); 1.9.x releases share one ABI
# (SONAME libgit2-*.so.1.9).
LIBGIT2_VERSION=1.9.7
LIBGIT2_LINK_NAME=git2-49e408b
#   in the package (runtimes/<rid>/native/)      the name the loader looks for
#   linux-*   libgit2-49e408b.so                  libgit2-49e408b.so.1.9 (its SONAME)
#   osx-*     libgit2-49e408b.dylib               @rpath/libgit2-49e408b.1.9.dylib (its install name)
#   win-x64   git2-49e408b.dll                    git2-49e408b.dll (no import library is in the
#                                                 package; build.dx.ps1 makes one from the DLL)
LIBGIT2_SONAME_LINUX=libgit2-49e408b.so.1.9
LIBGIT2_INSTALL_NAME_MACOS=@rpath/libgit2-49e408b.1.9.dylib
LIBGIT2_SHA256_LINUX_X64=ee82496311ae9d06534fb400654baeeacaee735911beaabcf14bace1b577840e
LIBGIT2_SHA256_LINUX_ARM64=8dd298a70646e3a427c13c84933e0e976dfcd8bf76315f4163a81d85431e3b5b
LIBGIT2_SHA256_WIN_X64=57e17c62ced01f96fd88dd041baee399f3102b09682ef7ad2dd1baa4c75dfd2d
LIBGIT2_SHA256_OSX_ARM64=6bb863400b6936781769206e0c711f710c0b2d4f94838fd7c1b9a2def2f1d1c7
LIBGIT2_SHA256_OSX_X64=70e449aafe5e4ec9c5a13b7f3fcc60d5bd67587aa3e480b560fb30b87402c158

# --- macOS and Windows ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The oldest macOS each slice must start on. Set EXPLICITLY (MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET for rustc,
# the linker and the C code) and ENFORCED: the macOS gate reads LC_BUILD_VERSION's minos from
# the binary and fails unless it equals this value. 11.0 is the first macOS on Apple Silicon;
# 10.15 is the floor CodeBrix.WebPlatform.WasmShrink's osx-x64 wasm-opt uses (Rust 1.100 itself
# supports x86_64 macOS from 10.12).
MACOS_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET_ARM64=11.0
MACOS_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET_X64=10.15
# Windows: the C runtime is linked statically (+crt-static, /MT for the C code in ring and zstd),
# so no Visual C++ Redistributable is needed; the gate fails on any VCRUNTIME / MSVCP /
# ucrtbase / api-ms-win-crt import. /PDBALTPATH:%_PDB% keeps the build folder's path out of the
# executable's debug directory.
WINDOWS_RUSTFLAGS="-C target-feature=+crt-static -C link-arg=/PDBALTPATH:%_PDB%"

# --- Files that travel with the binaries ------------------------------------------------------
# "<shipped name>:<path under tools/dx-native-tools, or @src/ for a path under TOOL_SOURCES_DIR>"
# Never a file named plainly LICENSE: these land in a consuming application's output folder.
#   LICENSE-Dioxus.txt        dioxus-cli is "MIT OR Apache-2.0", used under Apache-2.0; the
#                             crate as published carries no licence file, so this folder keeps
#                             one (licences/LICENSE-Dioxus.txt) - see README.txt
#   LICENSE-WasmBindgen.txt   wasm-bindgen-cli is "MIT OR Apache-2.0", used under Apache-2.0:
#                             the crate's own LICENSE-APACHE, verbatim
#   THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES-*.txt every crate compiled into each tool, with licence texts - GENERATED
#                             by `LibraryChecker notices --write` (README.txt, NOTICES)
LICENCE_FILES="LICENSE-Dioxus.txt:licences/LICENSE-Dioxus.txt
LICENSE-WasmBindgen.txt:@src/wasm-bindgen-cli/LICENSE-APACHE
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES-Dioxus.txt:notices/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES-Dioxus.txt
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES-WasmBindgen.txt:notices/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES-WasmBindgen.txt"

# --- Verification gate ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The highest GLIBC_x.y symbol version a shipped executable may reference. This IS the oldest
# system it can start on, so it is checked, not merely reported.
GLIBC_MAX_X64=2.28
GLIBC_MAX_ARM64=2.28

# Shared libraries a shipped executable may depend on: glibc and its loader, and libgcc_s.so.1;
# dx ALSO depends on libgit2 under its SONAME (LIBGIT2_SONAME_LINUX above) - it must, and
# wasm-bindgen must not.
# libgcc_s IS NEEDED by every Rust program for the gnu targets: the standard library's unwinder
# (panics unwind) links it by name, and -static-libgcc cannot remove it. It is GCC's runtime
# support library, part of the base system of every glibc distribution (glibc itself loads it
# for thread cancellation), so it is allowed and recorded. Everything else - OpenSSL, zlib,
# zstd, libssh2, any other libgit2, the C++ runtime - is forbidden: the C code dx contains (ring,
# zstd) is compiled in. The gate also fails if either program imports an OpenSSL symbol
# (OPENSSL_SYMBOL_PATTERN).
ALLOWED_DEPS="libc.so.6 libm.so.6 libpthread.so.0 libdl.so.2 librt.so.1 libgcc_s.so.1 ld-linux-x86-64.so.2 ld-linux-aarch64.so.1"
FORBIDDEN_DEP_PATTERN='^(libssl|libcrypto|libz\.|libzstd|libgit2|libssh2|libstdc\+\+|liblzma|libbz2|libcurl)'
OPENSSL_SYMBOL_PATTERN='^(SSL_|SSL_CTX_|OPENSSL_|EVP_|CRYPTO_|ERR_|X509_|BIO_|TLS_)'

# --- Functional gate (linux-x64 on the build host) ----------------------------------------------
# functional-gate.sh builds a SCRATCH COPY of this frontend (materialized: it carries the
# platform's crates in platform/vendor) with the built dx and wasm-bindgen, exactly as
# FrontendBuilder runs dx. The sample is the gate's deliberate subject: its frontend uses every
# part of the platform. Paths are relative to the repository root.
FUNCTIONAL_FRONTEND=samples/WorkSite/frontend
FUNCTIONAL_DX_ARGS="build --platform web --release --debug-symbols false"
# The wasm-opt the gate puts first on dx's PATH: the CodeBrix.WebPlatform.WasmShrink package's
# copy, as a consuming build gets it - from the local NuGet cache (NUGET_PACKAGES, else
# ~/.nuget/packages), at the version the FrontendBuilder library references:
#     <cache>/codebrix.webplatform.wasmshrink.apachelicenseforever/<version>/tools/wasm-opt/<rid>
# (a restore of the repository's solution puts it there). Override with WASM_OPT_DIR=<folder>.
FUNCTIONAL_WASMSHRINK_PACKAGE_VERSION=1.0.323.365
# The standalone Tailwind program the gate puts first on dx's PATH: the
# CodeBrix.WebPlatform.Styling.Tailwind4.<Platform> package's copy, as a consuming build gets it
# (through the host-tools package) - from the local NuGet cache, at the version the host-tools
# projects reference:
#     <cache>/codebrix.webplatform.styling.tailwind4.<platform>.mitlicenseforever/<version>/tools/tailwind/<rid>
# (a restore of the repository's solution puts it there). Override with TAILWIND_DIR=<folder>.
FUNCTIONAL_STYLING_PACKAGE_VERSION=1.0.327.82
# The tools the new ones are compared with, byte for byte: the programs committed for the RID
# (the ones in use until the new ones are adopted). Relative to the repository root; the gate
# skips the comparison, saying so, when the folder has no dx. Override with REFERENCE_TOOLS_DIR.
FUNCTIONAL_REFERENCE_TOOLS_DIR=native_libraries/linux-x64
